1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,137
Your new release is live.

2
00:00:02,137 --> 00:00:03,446
The home page loads.

3
00:00:03,446 --> 00:00:04,624
Everyone is happy.

4
00:00:04,624 --> 00:00:06,064
But the app is broken,

5
00:00:06,064 --> 00:00:07,177
and nobody knows.

6
00:00:07,727 --> 00:00:11,311
A home page that answers is not the same as an app that works.

7
00:00:11,861 --> 00:00:13,677
Today you build a health check,

8
00:00:13,677 --> 00:00:15,433
ship a bad release on purpose,

9
00:00:15,433 --> 00:00:16,897
see exactly how it fails,

10
00:00:16,897 --> 00:00:17,600
and recover.

11
00:00:18,150 --> 00:00:20,305
Watch, read, roll back, and fix.

12
00:00:21,855 --> 00:00:23,071
Four moves, in order.

13
00:00:23,621 --> 00:00:28,805
One: watch. A health page tells you the truth, and a small script asks it again and again.

14
00:00:29,355 --> 00:00:32,832
Two: read. When the health check fails, the log tells you why.

15
00:00:33,382 --> 00:00:34,503
Three: roll back.

16
00:00:34,503 --> 00:00:37,141
Put the last working release back first.

17
00:00:37,141 --> 00:00:38,262
Users come first,

18
00:00:38,262 --> 00:00:39,910
the investigation second.

19
00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:43,255
Four: fix the cause, and ship the release again.

20
00:00:43,805 --> 00:00:45,640
Watch, read, roll back, fix.

21
00:00:47,690 --> 00:00:52,575
Go to the lab folder. Type: cd /opt/lab111.

22
00:00:54,125 --> 00:00:56,024
Look around. Type: ls.

23
00:00:56,574 --> 00:00:59,539
Two release files.

24
00:01:00,089 --> 00:01:03,353
Cart 1.0 works. Cart 2.0 is new.

25
00:01:03,903 --> 00:01:07,573
The app reads its settings from a file.

26
00:01:08,123 --> 00:01:11,344
One setting: the database address. Remember this file.

27
00:01:11,894 --> 00:01:18,678
Start Tomcat, and keep the last line.

28
00:01:19,228 --> 00:01:27,996
Ask the health page.

29
00:01:33,146 --> 00:01:33,344
Up,

30
00:01:33,344 --> 00:01:34,731
version 1.0.

31
00:01:34,731 --> 00:01:37,968
A health page answers one question: are you well?

32
00:01:37,968 --> 00:01:39,289
It answers in words,

33
00:01:39,289 --> 00:01:40,677
and in a status code.

34
00:01:41,227 --> 00:01:43,552
A person can read that. A script needs a rule. Write a health script in Vim.

35
00:01:44,402 --> 00:01:45,858
Line one: the shell.

36
00:01:45,858 --> 00:01:47,460
Line two: the address,

37
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:48,480
in a variable.

38
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:51,465
Line three: ask for only the status code.

39
00:01:51,465 --> 00:01:51,829
Curl,

40
00:01:51,829 --> 00:01:52,339
dash S,

41
00:01:52,339 --> 00:01:54,232
dash O to the null device,

42
00:01:54,232 --> 00:01:56,562
and -w with the code format.

43
00:02:02,112 --> 00:02:03,869
Line four: print the code.

44
00:02:03,869 --> 00:02:05,559
Line five: the exit code.

45
00:02:05,559 --> 00:02:08,398
Success only when the code is two hundred.

46
00:02:08,398 --> 00:02:09,344
Save and quit.

47
00:02:13,894 --> 00:02:17,158
Make it executable.

48
00:02:17,708 --> 00:02:19,969
Run it.

49
00:02:20,519 --> 00:02:20,953
Health,

50
00:02:20,953 --> 00:02:21,696
two hundred.

51
00:02:21,696 --> 00:02:23,615
And the script exits with zero,

52
00:02:23,615 --> 00:02:24,853
which means success.

53
00:02:24,853 --> 00:02:26,834
Other tools can use that answer.

54
00:02:27,384 --> 00:02:31,629
Watch it, three times, one second apart.

55
00:02:39,479 --> 00:02:42,551
Steady. Now the risky part: ship release 2.0.

56
00:02:43,101 --> 00:02:49,309
Copy the new war file into the webapps folder, as cart.war.

57
00:02:49,859 --> 00:02:51,556
Tomcat watches that folder.

58
00:02:51,556 --> 00:02:54,385
In about two seconds it replaces the old app.

59
00:02:54,385 --> 00:02:55,705
Now watch the health.

60
00:02:56,255 --> 00:02:57,663
Watch the health, three times.

61
00:03:08,313 --> 00:03:11,961
Five oh three: service unavailable. The new release is sick.

62
00:03:12,511 --> 00:03:16,713
But look at the home page.

63
00:03:18,763 --> 00:03:20,279
CloudCart 2.0.

64
00:03:20,279 --> 00:03:21,268
The page loads!

65
00:03:21,268 --> 00:03:23,310
A customer sees a working site,

66
00:03:23,310 --> 00:03:25,156
while the health says: down.

67
00:03:25,156 --> 00:03:27,133
This is why we monitor health,

68
00:03:27,133 --> 00:03:28,385
and not only pages.

69
00:03:28,935 --> 00:03:30,385
Ask the health page what is wrong.

70
00:03:34,235 --> 00:03:34,555
Down,

71
00:03:34,555 --> 00:03:37,371
version 2.0: pool.size missing.

72
00:03:37,371 --> 00:03:40,699
The release needs a setting that does not exist yet.

73
00:03:41,249 --> 00:03:45,921
Now read the log. The app wrote the same message there.

74
00:03:47,571 --> 00:03:49,604
Setting pool.size is missing.

75
00:03:49,604 --> 00:03:50,467
In production,

76
00:03:50,467 --> 00:03:53,054
this line is what an alert would show you.

77
00:03:53,604 --> 00:03:54,664
Now the browser,

78
00:03:54,664 --> 00:03:56,453
the way a customer sees it.

79
00:03:56,453 --> 00:03:57,513
Open localhost,

80
00:03:57,513 --> 00:03:59,766
colon 8087,

81
00:03:59,766 --> 00:04:00,495
slash cart.

82
00:04:13,045 --> 00:04:15,459
A normal page: CloudCart 2.0,

83
00:04:15,459 --> 00:04:17,491
and the cart service is running.

84
00:04:17,491 --> 00:04:18,762
Nothing looks wrong.

85
00:04:23,312 --> 00:04:27,173
Now the health page: /cart/health.jsp.

86
00:04:35,723 --> 00:04:36,077
Down.

87
00:04:36,077 --> 00:04:37,560
Version 2.0.

88
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:39,114
Pool.size missing.

89
00:04:39,114 --> 00:04:39,679
One app,

90
00:04:39,679 --> 00:04:40,527
two answers.

91
00:04:40,527 --> 00:04:42,081
Trust the health page.

92
00:04:46,631 --> 00:04:52,903
Roll back now. Put release 1.0 back.

93
00:04:53,453 --> 00:04:56,077
Users first. The investigation can wait.

94
00:04:56,627 --> 00:04:58,035
Watch the health.

95
00:05:08,685 --> 00:05:11,607
Two hundred, three times. Recovered, in seconds.

96
00:05:12,157 --> 00:05:13,608
Ask the health page.

97
00:05:17,458 --> 00:05:20,445
Up, version 1.0. The old release is back.

98
00:05:20,995 --> 00:05:27,523
Now fix the cause. Add the missing setting.

99
00:05:28,073 --> 00:05:31,401
The double arrow adds a line at the end, and keeps the old one.

100
00:05:31,951 --> 00:05:33,444
Check the file.

101
00:05:35,894 --> 00:05:37,003
Two settings now.

102
00:05:37,553 --> 00:05:40,071
Ship release 2.0 again.

103
00:05:43,621 --> 00:05:45,733
Wait six seconds, then ask the health page.

104
00:05:56,083 --> 00:05:56,272
Up,

105
00:05:56,272 --> 00:05:57,597
version 2.0,

106
00:05:57,597 --> 00:05:58,607
pool equals ten.

107
00:05:58,607 --> 00:06:00,310
The same release works now,

108
00:06:00,310 --> 00:06:02,077
because the cause was fixed.

109
00:06:02,627 --> 00:06:08,131
One more proof. How many times was cart.war deployed?

110
00:06:09,381 --> 00:06:09,685
Four.

111
00:06:09,685 --> 00:06:11,202
Version one at the start,

112
00:06:11,202 --> 00:06:11,930
the bad two,

113
00:06:11,930 --> 00:06:12,780
the roll back,

114
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:13,629
and two again.

115
00:06:13,629 --> 00:06:15,632
Your log is your release history.

116
00:06:16,182 --> 00:06:18,998
Clean up.

117
00:06:22,548 --> 00:06:24,916
Stopped. Watch, read, roll back, fix.

118
00:06:26,466 --> 00:06:29,453
Your task. Ship a bad release, and recover from it.

119
00:06:30,003 --> 00:06:31,247
One: start Tomcat,

120
00:06:31,247 --> 00:06:32,975
and write health.sh,

121
00:06:32,975 --> 00:06:36,915
a script that exits with zero only on status two hundred.

122
00:06:37,465 --> 00:06:42,862
Two: ship cart 2.0. Watch the health turn 503, and read why in the log.

123
00:06:43,412 --> 00:06:46,804
Three: roll back to 1.0, and see two hundred again.

124
00:06:47,354 --> 00:06:51,514
Four: add pool.size to the settings, and ship 2.0 again.

125
00:06:52,064 --> 00:06:54,667
Then run lab check. All five must pass.

126
00:06:56,717 --> 00:07:00,920
Here is where to find everything. Pause on this screen if you need to write it down.

127
00:07:01,470 --> 00:07:03,118
The lab lives on GitHub,

128
00:07:03,118 --> 00:07:04,285
at it-waves,

129
00:07:04,285 --> 00:07:05,864
/devops-ai,

130
00:07:05,864 --> 00:07:07,375
in the folder academy,

131
00:07:07,375 --> 00:07:08,062
slash lab.

132
00:07:08,612 --> 00:07:14,073
Two files: lab111.sh, and shortcuts111.sh.

133
00:07:14,623 --> 00:07:15,546
Install it,

134
00:07:15,546 --> 00:07:18,481
on any Linux machine: sudo install,

135
00:07:18,481 --> 00:07:20,410
dash m seven five five,

136
00:07:20,410 --> 00:07:22,339
lab111.sh,

137
00:07:22,339 --> 00:07:23,597
into /usr,

138
00:07:23,597 --> 00:07:24,603
/local,

139
00:07:24,603 --> 00:07:25,610
/sbin.

140
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:31,173
Load the short name with: source shortcuts111.sh. You get: lab.

141
00:07:31,723 --> 00:07:32,849
You need a JDK,

142
00:07:32,849 --> 00:07:33,445
and curl,

143
00:07:33,445 --> 00:07:35,367
about one gigabyte of memory,

144
00:07:35,367 --> 00:07:40,335
and ports 8087 and 8097 free.

145
00:07:40,335 --> 00:07:41,792
No systemd is needed.

146
00:07:42,342 --> 00:07:44,310
Lab check tests your recovery.

147
00:07:44,310 --> 00:07:47,262
Lab down stops Tomcat and removes everything.

148
00:07:47,262 --> 00:07:49,361
Only run it on your own machine.

149
00:07:51,411 --> 00:07:53,480
Pass or fail. You need all six.

150
00:07:54,030 --> 00:07:55,844
Five pass lines in lab check.

151
00:07:56,394 --> 00:07:59,060
Tomcat answers on 8087.

152
00:07:59,610 --> 00:08:03,514
Your health script exists, and exits with zero when the app is healthy.

153
00:08:04,064 --> 00:08:07,328
Cart.war was deployed four times: 1.0,

154
00:08:07,328 --> 00:08:08,160
2.0,

155
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:09,056
the roll back,

156
00:08:09,056 --> 00:08:10,528
and 2.0 again.

157
00:08:11,078 --> 00:08:14,684
The log shows why health failed: pool.size is missing.

158
00:08:15,234 --> 00:08:18,818
Pool.size is set, and release 2.0 is healthy.

159
00:08:19,368 --> 00:08:20,669
To pass, all six.

160
00:08:22,719 --> 00:08:24,490
Your turn, on your own machine.

161
00:08:25,040 --> 00:08:27,770
One: lab up, read the task list, start Tomcat.

162
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:30,454
Two: write the health script, and watch it.

163
00:08:31,004 --> 00:08:33,350
Three: ship 2.0, and read the log.

164
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:35,927
Four: roll back to 1.0.

165
00:08:36,477 --> 00:08:39,485
Five: fix the setting, ship again, and run lab check.

166
00:08:40,035 --> 00:08:42,616
Six: lab down, and prove nothing is left.

167
00:08:43,166 --> 00:08:45,001
Pause the video, and begin now.

168
00:09:00,551 --> 00:09:01,426
Quick check.

169
00:09:01,976 --> 00:09:04,600
Why is a home page that loads not a health check?

170
00:09:05,150 --> 00:09:09,032
A release fails. What do you do first: roll back, or investigate?

171
00:09:09,582 --> 00:09:11,246
Where did you find why it failed?

172
00:09:11,796 --> 00:09:14,122
How does Tomcat notice a new war file?

173
00:09:14,672 --> 00:09:16,618
Objective covered: M C twelve,

174
00:09:16,618 --> 00:09:17,655
objective eight,

175
00:09:17,655 --> 00:09:18,823
health monitoring,

176
00:09:18,823 --> 00:09:21,093
and recovering a failed deployment.

177
00:09:21,643 --> 00:09:23,712
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

178
00:09:34,262 --> 00:09:36,673
Next: the Tomcat mastery scenario.

179
00:09:37,223 --> 00:09:40,551
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
